{"name":"Ambient Light","hex":"#c2e2e7","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ambient","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Encompassing on all sides; surrounding; encircling; enveloping.","Evoking or creating an atmosphere: atmospheric."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something that surrounds; encompassing material, substance or shape.","The atmosphere; the surrounding air or sky; atmospheric components collectively such as air, clouds, water vapour, hail, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ambient"},{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"}],"entries":[{"title":"Available light","blocks":[{"text":"In photography and cinematography, available light refers to any source of light that is not explicitly supplied by the photographer for the purpose of taking pictures. The term usually refers to light sources in the surrounding environment that are present naturally or artificial lighting that is already pre-existing. It generally excludes flashes, although arguably flash lighting provided by other photographers shooting simultaneously in the same space could be considered available light. Light sources that affect the scene and are included in the actual frame are called practical light sources, or simply practicals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg/330px-Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Available_light","wikidata":"Q168560"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg/500px-Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg/1280px-Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg/500px-Karaoke-irish-pub.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Available light used in a picture of a karaoke event in an Irish pub in Hamburg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKaraoke-irish-pub.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Fountain_and_Foliage.jpg/500px-Fountain_and_Foliage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Fountain_and_Foliage.jpg/500px-Fountain_and_Foliage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Example of photographic use of available light in the early 20th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFountain_and_Foliage.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip